request.prompt:
You are checking coaching feedback written about one football (soccer) instep kick. A
separate system measured the kick from video; "facts" is its output. "feedback" was written
from those facts, and "cited_sources" are the sources the feedback cites (empty if none).

Rate the feedback from 1 to 5 on each criterion:
- correctness: Is everything it says true to the facts and the sources it cites? 5 = Every statement matches the measurements and the cited sources; nothing invented. 3 = Mostly right, but one claim is unsupported, overstated or slightly off. 1 = Contradicts a measurement (e.g. says too bent when the number says too straight) or invents facts or sources. (2 and 4 are in between.)
- specificity: Is it about this kick, not football in general? 5 = Built on this kick's measurement and fault; it couldn't be pasted onto another kick. 3 = Names the fault, but the rest would fit any player. 1 = Generic advice that ignores what was measured. (2 and 4 are in between.)
- actionability: Could the player act on the cue and drill at their next session? 5 = A clear cue and a drill you could set up and do alone, without asking anyone. 3 = Understandable, but vague about what to do or how to practise it. 1 = No usable instruction. (2 and 4 are in between.)

Judge only what is shown. A claim that no fact or cited source supports counts against
correctness even if it is common coaching advice. Give a one-sentence reason.

{
 "facts": {
  "fault": {
   "name": "Plant foot far behind the ball",
   "feature": "plant_foot_offset",
   "value": -0.36,
   "unit": "leg_lengths",
   "fires_when": "below",
   "threshold": -0.25,
   "threshold_source": "heuristic: the extracted norms give the plant foot's position in cm (e.g. N-027, N-031), and converting needs a leg length in cm that a video doesn't give. A quarter of a leg length behind the ball centre is an uncalibrated guess, and this feature's accuracy has not been measured against labels (E2 doesn't score it)."
  },
  "measured": [
   {
    "id": "knee_angle_contact",
    "value": 137.23,
    "unit": "deg",
    "definition": "interior angle between thigh and shin at ball contact; 180 = straight"
   },
   {
    "id": "max_knee_flexion_backswing",
    "value": 71.22,
    "unit": "deg",
    "definition": "the smallest interior knee angle in the backswing (most bent); 180 = straight, so a larger number means a less bent knee"
   },
   {
    "id": "trunk_lean_contact",
    "value": 5.36,
    "unit": "deg",
    "definition": "trunk angle from vertical at contact; negative = leaning back"
   },
   {
    "id": "plant_foot_offset",
    "value": -0.36,
    "unit": "leg_lengths",
    "definition": "plant ankle's horizontal distance from the ball centre, in leg lengths; negative = behind the ball"
   },
   {
    "id": "segment_sequence_order",
    "value": "proximal_to_distal",
    "unit": "order",
    "definition": "whether the thigh's speed peaked before the shank's"
   }
  ],
  "not_measured": [
   "peak_knee_ext_velocity"
  ],
  "norm_comparison": [
   {
    "verdict": "no_comparable_norm",
    "norm_ref": null,
    "norm_mean": null,
    "norm_sd": null,
    "population": null
   }
  ]
 },
 "feedback": {
  "what": "Your plant foot landed behind the ball at a distance of -0.36 leg lengths.",
  "why_it_matters": "Landing too far back makes your body lean away from the ball and reduces your power.",
  "cue": "Step closer to the ball.",
  "drill": "Place a marker next to the ball and practice stepping beside it before you kick."
 },
 "cited_sources": []
}

